<div class="notice"><strong>Read these figures together, not as substitutes.</strong> International commodity prices can rise even when global cereal production remains high. Aggregate production cannot show whether a specific household can afford food or whether supplies reach a particular market.</div>
<h2>September 2026: what moved—and what it means</h2>
<p>The FAO Food Price Index averaged 136.0 points in September, up 1.5% month on month and 5.8% from September 2025. Fastmarkets' review of the release described this as the highest reading since November 2022. That comparison matters: the index has risen materially from a year earlier, but it remains an international commodity-price benchmark—not a direct measure of the price of every national grocery basket.</p>
<p>The headline conceals sharply different movements across food groups. Cereal and sugar prices rose more quickly than the all-commodity index, while meat and dairy were flat to lower. This composition helps explain why the price pressure felt by a bakery, an importer, a farmer and a household may differ.</p>
