<h2>Production, stocks and trade tell a second story</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Measure</th><th>Latest FAO figure</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Global cereal production, 2026</td><td>2,979 million tonnes; −2.1% from 2025</td><td>The second-largest harvest on record is not evidence that every market has reliable or affordable supply.</td></tr>
<tr><td>World cereal trade, 2026/27</td><td>505.8 million tonnes; forecast down 3.5% from 2025/26</td><td>Trade can tighten even when production remains historically high; shipping routes and export capacity matter.</td></tr>
<tr><td>World cereal stocks-to-use ratio</td><td>31.7%, down from 32.0%</td><td>A useful global buffer indicator, but aggregate stocks do not tell us where stocks are held, their quality, price or accessibility.</td></tr>
<tr><td>World cereal stocks at close of seasons in 2027</td><td>950 million tonnes, forecast broadly unchanged from opening levels</td><td>Stocks are a forecast and a global aggregate; they are not a warehouse inventory available to any one country.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>FAO's October brief links its lower trade outlook to constrained Black Sea shipping routes and insufficient alternative transport capacity. Its market note also flags freight rates, fertilizer markets and logistical disruption. Those are transmission channels to monitor—not proof that a nationwide shortage has occurred in every importing country.</p>
<h2>How to assess a food-crisis headline</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Identify the metric.</strong> Is the claim about an international price index, a country's consumer inflation, production, stocks, imports, or acute food insecurity?</li>
<li><strong>Check the place and period.</strong> Match the geography, crop year, reference month and publication date. Do not compare a global annual forecast with a local weekly shelf report as though they measured the same thing.</li>
<li><strong>Look for the transmission mechanism.</strong> For example: a wheat quotation change may affect import costs, then milling and wholesale prices, with timing dependent on contracts, currency, freight and local policy.</li>
<li><strong>Verify local consequences separately.</strong> Use official retail price data, market-arrival or supply reports, import data, and credible reports of persistent stockouts. One global indicator cannot confirm or rule out a local disruption.</li>
<li><strong>Recheck revisions.</strong> Forecasts and provisional inflation rates change. Preserve the source's original date when quoting a figure and check whether a newer release supersedes it.</li>
</ol>
<h2>What this data does—and does not—show</h2>
<p><strong>What it shows:</strong> September brought broad international commodity-price pressure, especially in cereals and sugar, while FAO still forecast the second-largest global cereal harvest on record. The trade outlook weakened at the same time. These facts can coexist because production, trade logistics, commodity prices and household access measure different links in the food system.</p>
<p><strong>What it does not show:</strong> these aggregate figures do not establish a universal global food shortage, nor do they prove that local shortages are impossible. Local evidence is needed for local claims, and household food security also depends on income, conflict, market access and assistance.</p>
